  "text": "The first thing adapters buy us is a way around that data problem at least for scale. Look at how fast the data dries up as the buildings get bigger: something like forty-seven thousand single apartments to learn from, but only around three thousand whole buildings. Way too few to imitate a building directly. So the trick is to let the adapters stand on each other's shoulders. We train the apartment adapter first, where there's tons of data. The floor adapter starts from everything the apartment one already knows, and only has to learn what's new about floors. The building adapter starts from both. The payoff: by building on what the smaller adapters learned, the building adapter has effectively seen something like SIXTY-FOUR thousand examples, instead of three thousand. That's how we get it to imitate buildings even when there's almost nothing to imitate.",
