  "sentences": [
    {
      "text": "The first thing adapters buy us is a way around that data problem at least for scale.",
      "start": 0.079,
      "end": 4.699
    },
    {
      "text": "Look at how fast the data dries up as the buildings get bigger: something like forty-seven thousand single apartments to learn from, but only around three thousand whole buildings.",
      "start": 5.9,
      "end": 14.599
    },
    {
      "text": "Way too few to imitate a building directly.",
      "start": 14.88,
      "end": 17.0
    },
    {
      "text": "So the trick is to let the adapters stand on each other's shoulders.",
      "start": 18.239,
      "end": 21.199
    },
    {
      "text": "We train the apartment adapter first, where there's tons of data.",
      "start": 21.42,
      "end": 24.42
    },
    {
      "text": "The floor adapter starts from everything the apartment one already knows, and only has to learn what's new about floors.",
      "start": 24.639,
      "end": 30.359
    },
    {
      "text": "The building adapter starts from both.",
      "start": 30.559,
      "end": 32.34
    },
    {
      "text": "The payoff: by building on what the smaller adapters learned, the building adapter has effectively seen something like SIXTY-FOUR thousand examples, instead of three thousand.",
      "start": 33.36,
      "end": 42.039
    },
    {
      "text": "That's how we get it to imitate buildings even when there's almost nothing to imitate.",
      "start": 43.2,
      "end": 46.939
    }
  ]
